# Metrics

s3gw does not emit metrics itself: the gateway reports every request to its
observer (`Gateway.SetObserver`) and exposes cache statistics
(`Gateway.SignerCacheStats`, `Gateway.ClientCacheStats`), and a service turns
those into metrics. This document is the convention those metrics follow, so
that dashboards and alerts built for one service built on s3gw work for
another. It is generated from the [Weaver](https://github.com/open-telemetry/weaver)
registry in `s3gw/semconv/model`, which is the source of truth; attributes
not prefixed `s3gw.` are [OpenTelemetry semantic conventions](https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/),
whose own definitions (and value lists) apply unless noted here.

`s3gw/otelmetric` implements it with the OpenTelemetry metrics API, and
`s3gw/semconv` holds the names as Go constants for services that implement it
themselves (with Prometheus, say, the names map to `s3gw_request_duration_seconds`
and so on through the usual OpenTelemetry-to-Prometheus translation).

## Cardinality

Metric attributes must stay bounded. The object key, the user, the access key
id and the request id are never metric attributes — they belong in the log,
where the observer's `RequestInfo` already carries them. `s3gw.tenant` and
`aws.s3.bucket` are opt-in: their cardinality is the number of tenants and
buckets, which is the service's to judge. The registry's policy
(`s3gw/semconv/policies`) enforces both rules.

